      "nets": ["REG_FB", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet the feedback network around REG1 (MIC26903YJL-TR) contains three resistors: R5 6k8 from the post-L1 output node to the REG_FB node, R6 1k3 from REG_FB to GND, and an additional R4 19k6 also connected from REG_FB to GND (i.e. R4 in parallel with R6).",
      "expected": "The intended divider is only R5 (top) and R6 (bottom), as stated by the schematic/spec note 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)' which with 6k8/1k3 yields 5 V. There should be no R4 across the bottom leg; the spec's power section lists only R5 and R6 for the feedback divider.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet (page 1): 'R4 19k6' wired between the REG_FB node and GND, alongside 'R6 1k3' and 'R5 6k8'; note 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "'feedback divider relation \"Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)\"' and '5 V rail: A MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V'"}
      ],
      "impact": "R4 in parallel with R6 reduces the effective bottom resistance to ~1.22 kΩ, so the buck regulates to 0.8*(1+6.8/1.22) ≈ 5.26 V instead of the intended 5.0 V, putting the 5 V servo/distribution rail about 5% overvoltage and violating the documented divider relation.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic otherwise matches the Hades specification (dual MCU, sensor set, INA219/PCA9685/EEPROM addresses, power topology, and the documented barometer pin swap), but the 5 V buck feedback divider contains an extra R4 19k6 placed in parallel with R6, which is not part of the intended Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6) network and would raise the regulated 5 V rail to roughly 5.26 V."
}

